name,kind,table_csv,params,lines,reference,notes
led_white_2733k,continuous,led_white_2733k.mietab,,,"CIE 015:2018 Table 12.1 std illuminant LED-B1 (phosphor-converted-blue, CCT~2733K); corroborated vs Cree XLamp XP-G3",phosphor-converted white LED SPD; colour-science dataset grep-verified
sc_superk,continuous,sc_superk.mietab,,,"NKT Photonics SuperK EXTREME datasheet (Jan 2011) p.4 'Spectral Power Density', EXR-20 curve; corroborated vs SuperK EXTREME/FIANIUM catalog ~2019-20 + SuperK EXTREME OCT datasheet","digitized supercontinuum SPD 400-2400nm (mW/nm rel.), +-20-30% visual digitization; 1064nm residual pump spike CLIPPED at plot top so 8.0 is a lower bound; clip to the bench's material-table window when tracing (docs/RAYTRACER.md)"
bb_halogen_3000k,blackbody,,temp_k:3000;lam_lo_nm:350;lam_hi_nm:2600,,"Thorlabs QTH10/QTH10-M Quartz Tungsten-Halogen lamp source datasheet (nominal ~3000K color temperature filament; typical usable QTH output range ~350-2600nm)","analytic Planck blackbody at 3000K synthesized to a dense SPD table at load (planck_relative_power); a real QTH lamp's output is additionally shaped by the quartz envelope/window transmission (rolls off below ~300nm and above ~2700nm) and the tungsten filament's non-unity, wavelength-dependent emissivity -- NEITHER is modeled here, so this is the bare 3000K Planck shape over the lamp's nominal range, not the true lamp+envelope emission product"
d2_uv_approx,continuous,d2_uv_approx.mietab,,,"Hamamatsu L2D2 series deuterium lamp datasheets (typical D2 continuum output range ~185-400nm, broad continuum hump peaking near 200-250nm); shape digitized/approximated from typical published D2 lamp spectral-output curves","smooth UV-CONTINUUM-ONLY approximation (skewed Gaussian hump, peak 235nm): the well-known deuterium Balmer-alpha/beta atomic emission lines at 486.0nm and 656.1nm that a real D2 lamp ALSO emits are DELIBERATELY OMITTED from this table -- use this row only where the broadband UV continuum (absorption-spectrophotometer reference channel) matters, not where the visible Balmer lines matter"
hg_penlamp,lines,,linewidth_nm:0.05,"253.65:1000;296.73:150;302.15:220;313.16:320;334.15:60;365.01:900;404.66:170;435.83:430;546.07:260;576.96:130;579.07:140","NIST Atomic Spectra Database (ASD) line list; Sansonetti, C.J., Salit, M.L. & Reader, J. 1996, J. Res. Natl. Inst. Stand. Technol. 101, 335 (wavelengths); relative intensities are illustrative typical Hg pen-lamp calibration-line strengths (cf. e.g. Newport/Oriel Hg(Ar) pen-lamp calibration-source technical notes)","standard 11-line Hg pen-lamp calibration set; 253.65nm (Hg resonance line) and 365.01nm are the two strongest lines, matching common pen-lamp datasheet call-outs; relative intensities are representative/typical, not a single-source absolute radiometric measurement; linewidth_nm=0.05 is a nominal low-pressure-discharge Doppler/pressure-broadening floor, not a measured linewidth"
